Exactly — less “a building full of animals,” more a social interface between humans who want animal connection and animals who could benefit from human connection.

The core object is the relationship, not the pet.

You could model it as:

Person ↔ Interface ↔ Animal

The interface handles identity, temperament, availability, location, trust, boundaries, and continuity. A person might open it and choose something like: “I want to walk a dog for 45 minutes,” “my kids want to play with a calm animal,” “I’d like a cat to sit with while I read,” or “I want to build a recurring relationship with the same dog.” On the animal side, the system knows: Milo likes adults, loves fetch, gets overwhelmed by toddlers, needs two walks today, already had three interactions, and has bonded with Brandon before.

So rather than simply matching available animal → available person, it builds a persistent relationship graph:

human ↔ animal ↔ owner/shelter ↔ place ↔ activity ↔ history

That creates some interesting modes. You could have Browse, where people discover animals nearby; Match, where the system recommends compatible pairings; Visit, for controlled interactions; Borrow, for vetted outings or temporary companionship; Regulars, where people and animals establish recurring relationships; and Home, where fostering or adoption becomes available when appropriate.

Crucially, the animal gets a kind of represented agency in the interface. Obviously it cannot operate an app, but caretakers and accumulated observations can encode things like likes, dislikes, fatigue, stress, preferred humans, preferred activities, and “not today.” An animal shouldn’t become an on-demand commodity just because someone clicked a button.

Then the deeper idea becomes almost a human–animal connection protocol.

Today, ownership is basically binary:

my animal / not my animal.

Your interface creates intermediate relationship states:

stranger → visitor → playmate → walker → regular → trusted caregiver → foster → family

And those states can exist without forcing everyone toward ownership.

That is much more interesting than a literal pet library. It is essentially infrastructure for sharing affection, attention, exercise, care, and companionship across the human–animal network.